26,-1.5497195136049413e-06,-4.0649541915627196e-05,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.04459652304649353,-0.03671035170555115,-1.9073468138230965e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.004982317797839642,-0.0005684667266905308,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.0728830375228426e-06,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.004556036554276943,-1.3351351299206726e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.025917427614331245,-1.9073468138230965e-06,-5.960462772236497e-07,-0.010966742411255836,-0.0005772831500507891,-0.036178164184093475,-0.009983132593333721,-0.000129691296024248,-7.986703712958843e-05,0.0,0.0,-4.768370445162873e-07,0.0,-2.3603161025675945e-05,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.00011884459672728553,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-1.2516897186287679e-05,-1.2755313036905136e-05,-3.40932747349143e-05,-3.933898824470816e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,10374,13233,90,64,2137,3,57960,10374,13233,90,65,2137,3,323,57960,10374,13233,90,66,31716,387,2326,4982,22879,11,1741,429,279,9210,1948,894,315,1105,374,279,29783,9210,57960,15976,2418,220,576,8123,315,279,27853,32507,291,2248,7907,553,1493,2326,22879,374,57960,37018,90,16,15170,59,26888,90,18,21,15,3417,2418,220,7379,198,78045,18,1124,9407,61,17,1124,15976,481,220,17,1124,9407,61,18,1124,15976,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ be three unit vectors, such that the angle between any of them is the acute angle $\\theta.$  The volume of the tetrahedron generated by these three vectors is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}.$  Find\n\\[3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"50a23365e77b7b315009d3f47fd85e1dc6a5cfe6a69ce0b811c9d95880bce246"},"seconds":28.13165545463562,"seed":822294657996257264,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the volume of the tetrahedron generated by the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) and use the given volume to find the value of \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\).\n\n### Step 1: Volume of the Tetrahedron\nThe volume \\(V\\) of the tetrahedron formed by vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) can be expressed using the scalar triple product:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right|\n\\]\nGiven that the volume is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Magnitude of the Cross Product\nSince \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) are unit vectors, the magnitude of the cross product \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left| \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} \\right| = \\sin \\theta\n\\]\nThus, the volume can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\right| = \\frac{1}{6} \\left| \\mathbf{a} \\right| \\left| \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} \\right| \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{6} \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Equating the Volumes\nWe equate this to the given volume:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{360}}\n\\]\nSolving for \\(\\sin \\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\theta = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{360}} = \\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Finding \\(\\cos \\theta\\)\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\right)^2 + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{10} + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 \\theta = 1 - \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{9}{10}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Calculating \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\)\nSubstitute \\(\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{9}{10}\\) and find \\(\\cos^3 \\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\cos^3 \\theta = \\left( \\frac{9}{10} \\right)^{3/2} = \\left( \\frac{9}{10} \\right) \\left( \\frac{9}{10} \\right)^{1/2} = \\frac{9}{10} \\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{27}{10\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{27\\sqrt{10}}{100}\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate \\(3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta\\):\n\\[\n3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 2 \\cos^3 \\theta = 3 \\left( \\frac{9}{10} \\right) - 2 \\left( \\frac{27\\sqrt{10}}{100} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{27}{10} - \\frac{54\\sqrt{10}}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{270}{100} - \\frac{54\\sqrt{10}}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{270 - 54\\sqrt{10}}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{27(10 - 2\\sqrt{10})}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{27(5 - \\sqrt{10})}{50}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{27(5 - \\sqrt{10})}{50}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{27(5 - \\sqrt{10})}{50}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:217"}
